11. LOW-2 resolution. The merge half of the round-1 dispute stands. The reclassification half did
    not: that path locks the journal LINES only, and its write takes FOR KEY SHARE on the target,
    which does not conflict with a concurrent createAccount's FOR KEY SHARE. `admitReclassification`
    now takes FOR UPDATE on the target when the leaf rule can bite it, proven with two pool
    clients (intruder INSERT -> 55P03 inside the guard's open transaction, nothing written). Both
    the accountMergeService comment and K-051's fix.md now say "merge path only" explicitly.

12. LOW-4. `isSubjectToLeafRule(level, accountType)` extracted into isReportVisibleLeaf and called
    by isReportVisibleLeaf itself, accountMergeGuards and accountParentRules — one statement of
    the rule in the module. The merge guard cannot call the whole predicate: it needs the target
    and source arms apart to word the refusal.
    LOW-5. isReportVisibleLeaf's docstring now records that validateLeafAccount is deliberately
    stricter, and why the two may disagree about an expense header.

── ROUND 4 (blind re-review 2cc28c2f: 2 MEDIUM, 3 LOW) ────────────────────────

13. MED-1. The target lock was unconditional, so `propose_reclassification` and a dry run held
    FOR UPDATE on the account for the length of a preview — the opposite of what planFromArgs's
    parameter doc and plan.ts's comment both say. `GuardInput.lockCandidateLines` is now required
    and gates the account lock, and `planFromArgs` translates GL_ACCOUNT_LOCK_TIMEOUT alongside
    RECLASS_LINE_LOCKED (uncaught it renders as GENERIC_FAILURE, naming nothing).

14. MED-2. `assertParentUsable` locks the parent FOR UPDATE before its posted-lines read. The
    one-sided lock only ordered the two transactions: createAccount decided before its INSERT took
    FOR KEY SHARE and never re-read, so the blocked INSERT resumed and committed the header anyway.

